WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:02.460
- [Instructor] We're told
Glen drained the water

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:04.440
from his baby's bathtub.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.610
The graph below shows the relationship

00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:09.510
between the amount of water
left in the tub in liters

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:12.540
and how much time had passed in minutes

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:14.940
since Glen started draining the tub.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.800
And then they ask us a few questions.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.300
How much water was in the tub
when Glen started draining?

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:21.900
How much water drains every minute?

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:22.980
Every two minutes?

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:25.650
How long does it take for
the tub to drain completely?

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:28.410
Pause this video and see if
you can answer any or all

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:31.740
of these questions based on
this graph right over here.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:32.860
All right, now let's do it together.

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:34.820
And let's start with this first question.

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:38.320
How much water was in the tub
when Glen started draining?

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:40.400
So what we see here is
when we're talking about

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.630
when Glen started draining,

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:44.880
that would be at time t equals zero.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.930
So time t equals zero is right over here.

00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:48.850
And then so how much water is in the tub?

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:50.590
It's right over there.

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:53.130
And this point, when
you're looking at a graph,

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:54.430
often has a special label.

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:56.040
If you view this as the y-axis,

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:57.840
the vertical axis as the y-axis

00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:59.940
and the horizontal axis as the x-axis,

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.050
although when you're measuring time,

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:03.120
sometimes people will call it the t-axis.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.600
But for the sake of this video,
let's call this the x-axis.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.600
This point at which you
intersect the y-axis

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.610
that tells you what is y when x is zero?

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:15.590
Or what is the water in
the tub when time is zero?

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:19.700
So this tells you, the y-intercept
here tells you how much,

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:24.700
in this case, how much water
we started off with in the tub.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.490
And we can see it's 15 liters

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:30.810
if I'm reading that graph correctly.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:32.510
How much water drains every minute?

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:33.630
Every two minutes?

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:34.463
Pause this video.

00:01:34.463 --> 00:01:36.480
How would you think about that?

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:38.970
All right, so they're
really asking about a rate.

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:42.110
What's the rate at which
water's draining every minute?

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:44.730
So let's see if we can find
two points on this graph

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:45.660
that look pretty clear.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:48.120
So right over there at time one minute,

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:51.250
looks like there's 12
liters in it, in the tub.

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:54.330
And then at time two minutes,
think there's nine liters.

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:58.740
So it looks like as one minute passes,

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.440
so we go plus one minute,

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:04.960
plus one minute, what happens
to the water in the tub?

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:08.730
Well, it looks like the water
in the tub goes down by,

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:11.100
went from 12 liters to nine liters.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:13.733
So negative three liters.

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:16.740
And this is a line, so
that should keep happening.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:19.720
So if we forward another plus one minute,

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:21.910
we should go down another three liters,

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:24.030
and that is exactly what is happening.

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:26.130
So it looks like the tub is draining

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:27.560
three liters per minute.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:28.500
So draining

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:34.983
three liters per minute.

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:37.750
And so if they say every two minutes,

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:40.160
well, if you're doing three
liters per every one minute,

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:42.290
then you're going to do twice
as much every two minutes.

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:47.290
So six liters every two minutes.

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:49.610
Two minutes.

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:51.070
But all of this, the second question,

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:53.770
we were able to answer
by looking at the slope.

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:56.230
So in this context, y-intercept
to help us figure out,

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:57.690
well, where did we start off?

00:02:57.690 --> 00:02:59.320
The slope is telling us the rate

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:02.930
at which the water in
this case is changing.

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:04.310
And then they ask us how long does it take

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:06.220
for the tub to drain completely?

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:09.100
Pause this video and see
if you can answer that.

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:12.070
Well, the situation in which
the tub has drained completely,

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:14.020
that means the there's
no water left in the tub.

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:16.340
So that means that our y-value,

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:18.490
our water value is down at zero.

00:03:18.490 --> 00:03:21.090
And that happens on the
graph right over there.

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:24.980
And this point where the
graph intersects the x-axis,

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:28.110
that's known as the x-intercept.

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:29.320
And in this context, it says,

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:33.930
hey, at what x-value do we not
have any of the y-value left?

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:35.880
The water has run out.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:38.470
And we see that happens
at an x-value of five,

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:40.660
but that's giving us the time in minutes.

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:43.080
So that happens at five minutes.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:46.890
After five minutes, all
of the water has drained.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:49.400
And that makes us a lot of sense.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:50.870
If you have 15 liters

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:53.120
and you're draining three
liters every minute,

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:55.710
it makes sense that it takes five minutes

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:58.083
to drain all 15 liters.

